In article <1995Apr23.160603.91288@kuhub.cc.ukans.edu>, halbhavi@tisl.ukans.edu (Sudhir Halbhavi) writes:
|>Hi 
|>
|> I would look to have more info on this debugger. I find the man
|>page on the Alphas in our department but DECladebug is not installed.
|>Is it not part of the standard source/binary?
|>
|>I would like to know how I can get this. Can anybody give me
|>info on this debugger as to where I can get it, how much it
|>would cost and also whether I could get the source with it?
|> Also  does it have a GUI?
|>

Sudhir,

   There are a number of ways you can get DECladebug.  The most common way 
is via as part of the Developer's Toolkit on the version 3.0 OSF/1 (now 
Digital UNIX) CD.  DECladebug is also shipped as part of the FUSE kit.  We've
also been known to show up in kits with new compilers.

   It's very likely that you already have the Developer's Toolkit.  

   DECladebug does have a GUI.  If you have a FUSE kit, you'll find a version
of DECladebug and a GUI there.  That DECladebug (and its GUI) can run outside
of FUSE.  At the present time, that's the only place we make the GUI 
available.  This may change in the near future; stay tuned.

   As far as I am aware, we do not make the sources available.  If you're
interested in knowing a little about the DECladebug internals, you can go
to the OOPSLA 93 proceedings.  There's a paper entitled "Experiences 
Developing and Using an Object-Oriented Library for Program Manipulation,"
by Tim Bingham, David Husson, and Nancy Hobbs.  It can tell you a lot about
DECladebug.

|> We are primarily interested in this since it has good support
|>for threads according to the man page and we could make use of a
|>debugger for our projects on threads. Also can some one give
|>me info on what features the future releases will have as regards
|>support for threads?
|>

   DECladebug has very good thread support starting in v3.0.  See the 
"Debugging Multithreaded Applications" chapter in the DECladebug 
documentation for full details.  It's a pretty good bet that further 
enhancements are in the works for upcoming releases.  What kinds of 
support are you looking for?

   DECladebug in version 3.0 and beyond also:

	- accepts the dbx command set
	- supports C, C++, FORTRAN-77, FORTRAN-90, Ada, and COBOL
	- attaches/detaches to/from running processes
	- comes with EMACS integration support
	- has significant performance improvements over 2.0
	- contains even more

   In particular, we've made significant improvements in our startup
times and our memory consumption.  We now do much better with large 
programs than we did on 2.0.  (We've seen some monsters, too - and some
of those monsters are now part of our regular testbed.)
