In article <1995Apr20.060817.13118@riksun.riken.go.jp>, 
ichihara@rikvax.riken.go.jp (Takashi Ichihara) asks:

|>    I'm now thinking to buy and install a turbo-channel J300 board
|>(http://www.dec.com/info/DECnews-UNIX/1205.txt.html ) on our AXP
|>work station.  Does this board (J300) work to capture NTSC video signal 
|>and transfer it into nv ?  What is the typical performance of the 
|>board ?  How may frames per second ?  Thanks for any information.

The J300 works fine with nv.  The hardware is capable of 30 fps
at full NTSC resolution (640x480), but because nv is doing software video
compression and is also throttling traffic to your requested data rate,
you are typically going to see less than that.  At the default 'medium'
resolution which I think is 320x240, I get 30 fps with the bandwidth
limiter cranked to the max of 1 Mb/sec, but that's not going to be
typical for wide area conferences.

There was a bug in the sources for the nv3.3beta which has been fixed, but
I'm not sure whether the updated sources have been released yet.  If you
are building this from source, I've included the diffs for the fix below.

*** 9,14 ****
--- 9,15 ----
   *    Written by Mark Prior           <mrp@itd.adelaide.edu.au>,
   *               Lance Berc           <berc@src.dec.com>,
   *           and Steve McCanne        <mccanne@ee.lbl.gov>
+  *    27-Feb-95 TFM   Fixed chroma packing bug for MM Services
   */

  #if defined(J300)
***************
*** 169,178 ****
        *yp++ = y | ((v >> 24) & 0xff000000);

        if (xmit_color) {
!           uv = (v >> 8) & 0xff;
!           uv |= (((v >> 16) & 0xff00) & 0xff);
!           uv |= (((v >> 24) & 0xff0000) & 0xff);
!           uv |= (((v >> 32) & 0xff000000) & 0xff);
            *uvp++ = uv ^ 0x80808080;
        }
      }
--- 170,179 ----
        *yp++ = y | ((v >> 24) & 0xff000000);

        if (xmit_color) {
!           uv =  (v >> 8) & 0xff;
!           uv |= (v >> 16) & 0xff00;
!           uv |= (v >> 24) & 0xff0000;
!           uv |= (v >> 32) & 0xff000000;
            *uvp++ = uv ^ 0x80808080;
        }
      }
***************


