In article <GRUNWALD.95Apr20075611@foobar.cs.colorado.edu>, 
grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu (Dirk Grunwald) asks:

|>On a related note, has anyone tried using the new PCI-based FullVideo
|>Supreme board for 'nv' and other MBONE tools? Allegedly, 'nv' supports
|>the DEC MMS, and the FullVideo will wotk with MMS. Anyone know about
|>performance? The FullVideo is pretty cheap ($800) compared to the J300
|>($3000).

I just tried this to make sure it worked as I expected and it works fine.
You don't even need to rebuild your nv binary if it was already built with
Multimedia Services for OSF/1 support.  Just plug in the FullVideo Supreme,
install MMS V1.5 which has the new hardware support, and you're ready to
run.

For nv, the performance of the FullVideo Supreme should be comparable to
the Sound & Motion J300 given comparable systems with comparable network
bandwith.  Because nv doesn't use the hardware JPEG accelleration on the
J300, its performance isn't effected by the fact that it's not included
on the FullVideo Supreme.

The principal things which changed from the J300 to the FullVideo Supreme are:

	- TurboChannel interface replaced by PCI
	- audio support removed 
	  (CD quality audio is available separately & is usually bundled)
	- JPEG compress/decompress hardware assist removed
	- no S-Video connectors
	- single composite video input instead of 2 S/W selectable inputs

It still does composite input and output at full video rates for
SECAM (input only), PAL, & NTSC video standards.  It still includes 
Digital's patented AccuVideo (tm) rendering technology.

There was an article posted to biz.digital.articles last week with the
details on both the FullVideo Supreme and the Multimedia Services release
which supports it if you need more information.  The board is supported
on any of the AlphaStations from 100 MHz up to 266 MHz.  It hasn't been
tested on other manufacters AlphaGeneration workstations, but I don't
know of any reason that it wouldn't work.

