: At this point the client "sees" the server in some general sense - it
: returns the correct information if I ypcat the password file - but it
: can't actually do anything with it, i.e., it wouldn't let me log on
: or change the password for any of the accounts defined on the server.

We've been running an NIS server under OSF/1 3.0 and 3.2, with OSF/1 3.0
and Ultrix 4.4 clients.  The message about 'SunOS servers rejected' is
a red herring -- you can safely ignore it.

I take it that you either ran 'nissetup' to get /etc/svc.conf configured
correctly or you edited it yourself?  It should have 'yp' listed as
a service for all of the databases you are serving with NIS, on both the
server (if you intend to allow NIS-served accounts to be used there) and
the clients.

You are using yppasswd to change passwords, correct?

If you set up NIS yourself, you may want to consider going back and
running 'nissetup'.  It's fairly foolproof.

