I've been reading with interest the discussion on advfs stability.

I have a question on performance.

Either running db tests or home grown C tests of advfs/ufs, I have noticed
the following behaviour.

		advfs	ufs
wall clock	lower	
system time		lower
# phys I/O	lower	

Single random decent (fill file system with data blocks intersperced with
directory creation and then a read verify):
	ufs:   elapsed 1758.216667  user 159.69456   system 76.417872
	advfs: elapsed 1450.300000  user 151.566944  system 101.845600
[Similar results for multiple concurrent executions of same test]

MUMPS database perf test (the db is the only file in the file system) in 
csh time format:
	ufs:   196.81u 16.47s 6:27 55% 0+47k 2280+1io 0pf+0w
	advfs: 197.39u 19.32s 6:00 60% 0+47k 8+0io    2pf+0w
	
I'm assuming better read caching (and perhaps coalescing) is producing the
lower wallclock and physical request statistics for advfs, but I was surprised
to see the system time increase so significantly.

Also, are there any gotchas to running a database (running with fsync() of
course) on an advfs filesystem ?

thanks in advance

tony
