>>>>> "T" == T Osborn <t.osborn@uea.ac.uk> writes:
In article <3n349g$s3e@cpca3.uea.ac.uk> t.osborn@uea.ac.uk (T.Osborn) writes:

Tim> Is AdvFS stable?

In general, yes.  We've been Beta testing Digital Unix (DEC OSF/1)
since 1.3 and in general it's been pretty solid.  

Tim> Do AdvFS and UFS run side-by side OK?

Yup.

Tim> Has any one created large single filestores eg 15GB of space from
Tim> a number of seperate disks? i.e. file systems which span multiple
Tim> physical disks

We haven't done this with any "big" partitions, but I do have one
system with two 400Mb partitions on seperate disks linked together.
It was the quickest easiest thing to do.

Tim> In general any comments would be appreciated.

If you want to run quotas on your advfs disks, it can be a pain to do.
We ended up copy /sbin/init.d/quota to /sbin/init.d/vquota and making
the changes to use the 'v'quota versions off the commands.  We then
had to add a new entry to /etc/rc.config and a link in /sbin/rc3.d
back to /sbin/init.d to have quotas start up properly.  Not all that
hard, just annoying.

Also, if the system does crash, reboots are really really really fast,
much better than UFS filesystems.  This is why we moved over to advfs
in the first place.

While making clone filesets is nice for doing dumps, they take a
disproportionate amount of time to delete once you are done with them. 

If you are using your disks for user home directories, keep in mind
that you will probably want to defragment the disks once a week to
keep up their access performace.  Unfortunately, defragment seems to
have problems with quotas enabled.  It's quite strange.

As another poster said, disk tools for fixing advfs filesystems are
lacking, though I would take a look at /usr/field/ at the programs:
msfsck, tag2name, vchkdir.  No man pages for these though.

There is no man page for advfsstat, which makes it hard to understand
what the numbers it gives mean, and how to adjust them for improved
performance.  Nor is mount_advfs documented either, since I'd like to
mount my partitions with more memory buffers.

One thing I really miss from Ultrix is rzdisk, which was great for
scanning disks for problem blocks, formatting disks, etc.  Scu just
doesn't seem to cut it as well.

In general, I'm very happy with advfs and would recommend it to
anyone.  If you have large disks, the time savings over UFS fsck is
well worth any other minor quibbles.

John

