siggy@aed.aed-graphics.de (Siggy Hensel) writes:

>In article <3n1dng$8js@post.gsfc.nasa.gov>,
>William Thompson <thompson@orpheus.nascom.nasa.gov> wrote:
>>In an earlier article I asked for some help trying to mount a Seagate ST15150N
>>drive in a DEC StorageWorks BA353 box.  Thanks to those who responded.  I've
>>now found out what the problem was.
>>
>>When I configured the drive to put in the StorageWorks box, I disabled the
>>internal terminator, because the BA353 cabinet is already terminated.  This was
>>the correct thing to do.  However, what I didn't appreciate was that I also
>>needed to reconfigure the drive so that it would supply power to the external
>>terminator.  When I changed the jumper setting from "Term. power from drive" to
>>"Term. power to bus", the problem disappeared.


>Is the StorageWorks box the last SCSI device in the chain?
>How is it terminated? With an external passive Terminator plug?

>I thought the BA353 does not need any passive terminator, because it is
>terminated automatically with an internal active termination circuit,
>if it is the last drive in the chain?

>(I think that's why the connectors are labelled INPUT and OUTPUT)

Yes, and I was depending on the internal terminator within the BA353 cabinet.
I also tried moving the Seagate drive to a different non-DEC cabinet (I think
off a Mac) with an external terminator but that didn't make any difference
either.  The Seagate people told me that an active terminator doesn't mean that
it doesn't need to have power supplied to it.  Changing the terminator power
jumper fixed the problem both with the BA353 and with the other box.

In any case, I'm now junking the BA353 box because I've found out that it
doesn't have enough cooling capacity for the ST15150N drive, or for DEC's
equivalent RZ29B-VA drive.  I just thought I'd mention that in case anyone else
has these drives in BA353 cabinets or other cabinets with inadequate cooling.

Bill Thompson



