From: chriscj@cat.com (system PRIVILEGED account)
Subject: HP-UX secure password aging and NIS serving password maps to DEC broken
Date: 1995/04/21
Message-ID: <3n8m5p$pb@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 101359700
organization: University of Illinois at Urbana
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.ultrix,comp.sys.hp.hpux,comp.sys.dec.comp.security.unix

Recently on our HP-UX network which serves DNS and NIS to 
not only HP-UX hosts, but hosts running Ultrix 4.4, and OSF/1 3.2A
the HP admin installed secure password aging. As a result of the 
installation NIS passwording on the DEC boxes broke. The reason
became apparent because of the HP password salt which added a 4
character age salt to the password field in /etc/passwd. The
password programs on the DEC boxes could not pass this information
along, and all users who depeneded on NIS password maps could not
login. I was wondering if anyone had any ideas on how to tackle
this issue. Right now, we've hacked it so that users on the DEC
side have a different password file, but can still have their
home directories mounted on any machine DEC or HP. This is a nuisance,
and was something that NIS was designed to clean up.

The pwd.h file defines the passwd fields to the HP to be

struct passwd {
               char    *pw_name;
               char    *pw_passwd;
               int     pw_uid;
               int     pw_gid;
               char    *pw_age;
               char    *pw_comment;
               char    *pw_gecos;
               char    *pw_dir;
               char    *pw_shell;
               long    pw_audid;
               int     pw_audflg;
           };

Whereas the pwd.h file defines the passwd fields on the DEc side to be:

struct  passwd { /* see getpwent(3) */
             char    *pw_name;
             char    *pw_passwd;
             uid_t    pw_uid;
             gid_t    pw_gid;
             int      pw_quota;
             char    *pw_comment;
             char    *pw_gecos;
             char    *pw_dir;
             char    *pw_shell;
     };

The only difference being in the pw_age filed on the HPUX side. 
My only solution that has since come to mind is to acquire the source
and library files to the HP-UX NIS passwd program, hack it to run on the
DEC side and go from there. It's a long shot at best, and right now
we need to run the HP password aging due to some administrative policy.
So any suggestions to get this working would be appreciated.

Chris
--
MIS Intern
Caterpillar LEC
West Lafayette, IN




