dpurnell@jove.ssu.umd.edu (J. David Purnell) writes:
>Do I need to configure the Alpha's serial port somehow?  The printer's serial
>port is running at 9600, the printcap entry specifies 9600.  Is it possible
>that the Alpha's serial port is running at some other rate regardless of 
>the printcap entry?

It might be as simple as a wrong cable.  I got the experience that DEC
serial ports sometimes need TxD/RxD reversed.  That is pin 2 and 3 on a
9 pin D-Sub connector.  Takes 5 minutes and a soldering iron.

Before trying to print via lp, or going down to the metal, check the
low level data flow.  That is, does "date > /dev/tty00" (or your
appropriate device) work?  You might hook up a serial terminal (like a
VT series or a Wyse one) to the port and check by the same procedure.

On a side note:  If you have parallel interfaces, or even a BNC connector
	on your printer ("JetDirect Card") I strongly suggest you use these
	rather than the painfully slow serial line.  Otherwise the printer's
	nice speed gets wasted by transmission time.


Greetings, Michael
