>drive or are there certain limitations. Certainly there`s someone with
>3rd party disk attached to their alphas. Can you give me a hint?
>
OSF/1 workds quite nicely with third party disk drives which have 512 byte
sectors and identify themselves as device type 0 (= hard disk).

I was unsuccessful in getting an NEC 3Xi CDROM drive to work on an 2000/300
under V1.3 and V2.0, nor was I able to get anyone on the net to indicate 
success in running an NEC CDROM under OSF/1.  I switched to a DEC CDROM before
V3.0, so can't speak to more recent versions of OSF/1.  The primary problem
with the NEC drives is that they default to 2048 byte blocks.  My attempts at
changing the cam_data.c file to force putting the drive into 512 byte mode did
not succeed in getting the drive to work under OSF/1.  The symptoms changed a
bit, but no success.  You might also try using the "scu" utility to put the
drive into 512 byte mode.

If you succeed, there are others on the net who would be interested, so please
post if you find a solution.

Given the hassle and the uncertainty whether one could rely on a third party
CDROM as an emergency boot device, you might want to consider paying DEC's
bloated price for "their own" drive.  At least they gave up trying to sell
slow seek, single speed drives for over $600; now you only have to pay about 
twice the price of a third party equivalent.



