>> This, of course, forces a reboot.  My question for people is whether this
>> is an indication of memory problem, or a cache problem.  One is easy to
>> solve, the other a lot more difficult.

>The only time I've seen such funny messages was after a mainboard had
>been replaced with an even more broken one...
>So IMHO you probably have got some hardware problems, at your place I'd
>contact the local support center.

>regards,
>Eric

I was receiving these invalid read from memory errors on my DEC3000 model 
300 a while back. It was causing total havoc, since the machine was 
rebooting several times a day. First we took it down to the >>>console  
prompt and did memory tests (which failed every time). This turned out to 
be a bug in the memory test due to having MOP set to ON. Turn MOP off 
with the set mop off command then reperform the memory test. Then you 
should get a proper memory test.

Next DEC replaced the motherboard and the processor which did indeed fix 
the problem. I asked for DEC to try and tell me what they thought was 
wrong, but they never got back to me.

Try getting DEC to replace your mainboard and processor, this should fix 
the problem.
--
