In article <3n349g$s3e@cpca3.uea.ac.uk>, T.Osborn (t.osborn@uea.ac.uk) wrote:

> We're beginning to use AdvFS, and are just enquiring whether people have 
> suffered from any problems. 

> Is AdvFS stable?

In our experience, no.

We experience daily crashes from AdvFS filesystem inconsistency problems,
generally during backups using cloned file systems. 

Digital has been working agressively on this problem for about two
months now, and while they have been in making our system
more stable, they're still chasing some elusive bugs. 

This is a 5000 user, Internet access machine, running OSF 3.0,
that is pounded upon heavily. Your experience, and others, may vary.

> Do AdvFS and UFS run side-by side OK?

Yes.

> Has any one created large single filestores eg 15GB of space from a number of
> seperate disks? i.e. file systems which span multiple physical disks

We have a 6 gigabyte disk structure that spans multiple disks. It works
as well as anything else, but keep in mind that if you lose 1 disk,
you need to restore the entire structure.

I'll note that we have chosen not to back out of AdvFS and replace it
with UFS, as we think that AdvFS is still a net benefit.

Other AdvFS issues that we've not sucessfully resolved:

	- On AdvFS volumes that consist of large numbers of small files,
	  it's not possible to determine reliably how much usable space
	  is left
	- vdump/vrestore will not restore quota information. If you rely
	  on quotas, be prepare to rebuild your quota files by hand or by
	  script. Do ask Digital "What is the documented and supported
	  procedure for backing up and restoring an AdvFS volume so
	  that you create a logically identical volume?". 
	- AdvFS diagnostic tools are very, very weak. If you get into any
	  trouble, there isn't a safety net of tools that you can depend
	  upon to rebuild disks or resolve problems. The tools are there,
	  undocumented, unsupported, and, in our experience, crash our
	  system.
	- AdvFS tuning is nonexistent.

	- Saul
