In article <3n349g$s3e@cpca3.uea.ac.uk>, T.Osborn <t.osborn@uea.ac.uk> wrote:
>
>We're beginning to use AdvFS, and are just enquiring whether people have 
>suffered from any problems. 
>
>Is AdvFS stable?

I'm my experience, yes.  However, this is on machines used for
research with less than 10 interective logins and only a few major
jobs running in the background.  But, we've only been running AdvFS
for a few months.  Your milage may vary.

>Do AdvFS and UFS run side-by side OK?

Yes.

>Has any one created large single filestores eg 15GB of space from a number of
>seperate disks? i.e. file systems which span multiple physical disks

Yes. We have a 4G partition made up of 4 disks.  This works quite
well.

>In general any comments would be appreciated.

In fact, early yesterday morning an over-enthusiastic cleaning person
managed to knock the power cord out of the above machine.  In addition
to the 4G partition above, it has a ufs root, and two more file
domains for /usr and /usr/local.  The machine came back up *very*
quickly... The longest part of the boot process was waiting for all 6
drives to spin up. If the data had been on ufs filesystems I would
have spent quite some time fsck'ing the disks.

I still have some reservations about advfs.  The tools are weak.
Vdump, in particular (no rvdump, can't disable posixism of updating
file access times when they're dumped).  And if you loose one disk in a
multi-disk file-domain you'll need to restore the entire thing.

