In article <Pine.OSF.3.91.950419104826.8485M-100000@stout.hitl.washington.edu>,
Jon Howell <jonh@hitl.washington.edu> writes:
jonh>     1. Why doesn't OSF/1 prototype malloc() and friends?

It does:

dsr_lns101% egrep 'malloc|free|calloc' /usr/include/stdlib.h 
extern void     *calloc __((size_t , size_t ));
extern void     free __((void *));
extern void     *malloc __((size_t ));

right where they are supposed to be, according to ANSI section 7.10.3.

jonh>     2. How do I find out more about C++ symbol-munging? I'd like
jonh>     to know the hows and whys, and how I can say in a prototype
jonh>     that this is a boring old C function, so don't munge the
jonh>     symbol name.

The how and why is that because of overloading, and the desire for
type-safe linkage, the mangled name of the function has to include
information about the argument types.  To tell the compiler that it is
a C function, declare 'em as

extern "C" {
    void *malloc(size_t size);
    void *calloc(size_t num_of_elts, size_t elt_size);
    void *realloc(void *pointer, size_t size);
    void free(void *pointer);
}

This gets done for you in /usr/include/stdlib.h, but is obscured
somewhat by the use of the _BEGIN_CPLUSPLUS/_END_CPLUSPLUS macros to
do it.  If you *really* want all the hows and whys, read the Annotated
Reference Manual.

Anyway, you shouldn't be using malloc/free unless you absolutely can't
avoid them--use new/delete instead.
-- 
