From: George Chaltas <chaltas@fluid.mro.dec.com>
Subject: Re: Reading TLZ06 on TLZ04 ?
Date: 1995/04/14
Message-ID: <3mm5l9$hu@mrnews.mro.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 100431466
references: <3mgvfd$m2t@micky.ibh-dd.de>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation - Marlboro, MA
newsgroups: comp.unix.ultrix,comp.unix.osf.osf1

beck@duck.ibh-dd.de (Andre Beck) wrote:
>
> Hi,
> 
> In the last time I'm getting software by other people on 4mm DAT tapes 
> which seem to be written using a TLZ06 or thelike compressing DAT drive.
> The only DAT drive I have is a TLZ04, which interestingly doesn't reject
> the compressed tapes in the first place, but instead feeds me with data
> of the expected entropy near 1.0 :-|
> 
>....


It may not help you out that much, but TLZ06 drives can be told
NOT to compress when the tape is created.  On DEC OSF/1 (Digital
UNIX), this is accomplished by writing to the l (the letter l)
variant of the the device, like this:

	tar cvf /dev/rmt0l myfile

When written in this manner, the tape should be readible on
a TLZ04, *provided* it's a 60m tape.  TLZ04 drives predate
the availability of 90M and 120M tapes, and have trouble handling
them (longer tapes typically mean thinnner tapes, and various
differences in both their physical and magnetic characteristics.

		George Chaltas



