Subject: Re: RAID, AdvFS & LSM
Date: 1995/04/14
Message-ID: <1995Apr14.202124.1@orca.alaska.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 100431493
distribution: world
references: <797724246snz@osil.demon.co.uk>
organization: University of Alaska
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

In article <797724246snz@osil.demon.co.uk>, dherdman@osil.demon.co.uk (Dave G Herdman) writes:

> There doesn't seem to be much pre-sales info available in the
> UK. It seems to me that there are several overlapping options:

Uh, everywhere (not just UK).... but you should get response
from here.  I'm responding to the list not you directly as
we just went through part of this exercise and grabbed info
from a variety of sources (Decus was among the best) and
more info back then would have helped us alot.

> 1  SWX controller on the 2100
> This would give us RAID-0, RAID-1, RAID-0+1 or RAID-5. Based on 
> VMS shadowing experience the temptation is to go for RAID-0.

I personally don't like volume sets / striping (Raid-0) under 
any of the architectures I've worked with: you lose one disk you 
lose them all.  Disks are low enough cost now that for applications 
requiring any level of recoverability Raid-0 isn't acceptable.
Depends on your applications, but if resilience is one of your 
goals, *don't* use Raid-0.

> What else should I consider I have heard that RAID-5 gives the
> best combination of disk use & resilience 

We looked at doing Raid-0+1 due to some performance concerns
with Raid-5 (write performance) and an unclear definition of
our application (it's new, we don't know it yet).  However, we
found that Raid-0+1 required a partial host implementation vs.
implementation within the controller (we use HSZ40) and that
opened another performance concern with the host.  The write-back
cache option for the HSZ40 alleviated our concern for write
performance under Raid-5... time will tell (application isn't
quite live yet, but looks good so far).  We have had two
disk psuedo-failures under Raid-5... the recovery was so good
and so transparent that we didn't even notice the one for
at least a week.  This is a hint that you need to add
monitoring procedures as nothing appears to be reported to
either the HSZ40 FMU or to OSF/1's UERF on a disk failure
(since to OSF/1 there *isn't* a failure).  Somebody with more
knowledge can correct me... but I suspect a future firmware
revision to the HSZ40 can "fix" this.
We did reserve disks within a spareset for automatic rebuild.
So far, I like what I see with Raid-5... we are using for
all partitions, typically in sets containing 3 or 6 RZ28's.

There was a learning curve (and still is) for configuring
the HSZ40.  OSF/1 v3.0 does not support dual redunancy in
the HSZ40's, I believe v3.2 does (we're not there yet).

> 2 LSM / LVM
> I understand that LVM is obsolete and is replaced by LSM. Is this
> correct. I cant get much detail on LSM. I am familiar with the HP/UX
> LVM. Is LSM similar ? What does it give me. Also is LSM a layered
> product or does it ship with OSF/1?

I believe you are correct, but cannot speak from firm knowledge.

> 3 AdvFS ?
> I think this is a journaling file system (like Veritas?). Is this
> true? How might I use this to good effect with the stuff above?. 

Use it...  no more fsck with AdvFS.
In addition (I think in conjunction with LSM), it provides much
more managability for partition sizing and expansion than you have
under UFS.  We implemented AdvFs for everything.  If you happen
to utilize a database which encourages use of raw devices you
likely wouldn't want AdvFS for that.  We checked with Oracle
before we implemented AdvFS for database files (non-raw, for us).
One note from our implementation of it... directory files became
8192 bytes which made the first attempt on converting a fairly full 
partition fail (if you have many users, you have lots of directories).

I'm still new with Unix (my experience is VMS and MVS, and several 
other retired architectures), so some real Unix heavies may have 
more to say pro/con.

> thanks in advance

good luck.
kurt carlson, u of alaska



