The report in my last summary that the crashes were due to a faulty
disk was only partially correct.  Some hangs were in fact due to the
faulty disk, but since that disk was replaced it has become apperant
that the machine was also hanging due to a different problem.

The OSF/1 team at DEC helped me diagnose the problem with Digital Unix
3.2 and narrowed it down to nfsd allocating too much memory to mbuf's
and causing idleproc to accumulate too much cpu time.  We had been
running 12 nfsd's (as this is our main nfs server), and the mbuf's
increased to about 29,000,000 bytes, where it leveled off, and I
rebooted the machine (so it wouldn't crash).  I don't know how much
memory mbuf has taken when the machine finally hangs.

I reduced the number of nfsd's running to the default of 8.  mbuf has
leveled of with 12,973,056 bytes of memory.  Our other alpha machines
have between 9000 and 20000 bytes allocated to mbuf, but they are not
running any nfsd's.  Another machine on campus (a DEC2100_A500) which
is running OSF/1 3.0 and 8 nfsd's has only 63744 bytes allocated to
mbuf's.

Anyways, the OSF/1 networking team is working on this problem, but I
was wondering if anybody else is having problems with nfs and 3.2,
also I would be interested in knowing how much memory is allocated to
mbuf's on other peoples 3.2 machines (use vmstat -M).

Thanks.
-- 
