oberman@icaen.llnl.gov writes:

>In Article <3mc0mg$j57@amhux3.amherst.edu>
>pdchapin@unix.amherst.edu (PAUL D. CHAPIN) writes:
>>After some of you have had time to play with Satan, the rest of use
>>probably would be interested in what you find.  Frankly, I'm not wild
>>about running a program whose stated purpose is to find holes in
>>system security until it has some kind of track record.

>An excellent suggestion. CIAC and CERT have already posted that running SATAN
>will CREATE a security problem on the system running it. This is supposed to be
>fixed by the yet-to-be-released V1.1 code. The problem is only present while
>SATAN is running, but anyone running courtney or any other intrusion detector
>could well exploit the hole while SATAN is still running.

One thing SATAN does is send 1 byte UDP messages to a lot of UDP ports,
including those used by NFS.  This elicits a number of error messages ala:

   xdr_mbuf: int crosses mbufs!
   clntkudp_callit_addr: inmbuf too short

This first is really a lie - the code assumes that the rest of an int is
in subsequent mbufs.  It isn't.  The latter can occur long after SATAN
has finished when NFS clients use a socket and notice an invalid message
on the input side.

Neither of these confuse NFS, but NFS is more talkative than most programs
about such stuff and tends to get blamed for reporting it.

I haven't taken a very close look at the tcpdump trace I collected, but
there doesn't appear to be anything very noteworthy.  SATAN looks like
a handy tool, but not much more.
