From: werme@alingo.zk3.dec.com (Eric Werme)
Subject: Re: Stale file handle?
Date: 1995/04/18
Message-ID: <werme.798176244@alingo.zk3.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 101096490
references: <saunders-1304952002390001@caspar.che.wisc.edu>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
reply-to: werme@zk3.dec.com
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

saunders@castor.che.wisc.edu (Brian Saunders) writes:

>Apr 12 19:22:55 castor vmunix: filehandle & stable flag not in first mbuf

This happens when virtual memory gets tight and the system is out of large
mbufs.  This message is from a third-order optimization in the NFS write
path.  I've deleted the message in OSF/1 V3.2.  (That it reported the
stable flag says you're using NFS V3, so that means you are running OSF/1
V3.0.  Time to upgrade!)

>Apr 12 19:37:58 castor vmunix: NFS server: stale file handle fs(8,2054) file 196
>820 gen 797216021
>Apr 12 19:37:58 castor vmunix:  getattr, client address = 128.104.170.38, errno
>22

Stale file handles occur when one client is accessing a file but the server
or some other client deletes it.  Usually no big deal, but they can be
annoying.

>Now, this IP address is an IRIX system that we export our user files to. 
>Is that a problem as well?

The stale file handles aren't, but if you "upgrade" to IRIX 5.3, that has
an massively broken NFS V3 implementation.  File locking won't work on
SGI's NFS V3 for some time.

>In addition, we will be making one of our systems doing a majority of the
>file exporting in the future.  Should we be adding to the number of nfsd
>and nfsiod daemons that are running?

The nfsiods are client-side performance aids, running more on the server won't
do anything for you.  If you expect the server to be really busy, then
16 or 24 nfsd might make sense, but I wouldn't bother to change from the
suggested 8 unless you see all the nfsds busy.
-- 
