Yes, LVM is being retired.  For details on Digital products, bring up
http://www.digital.com and/or http://www.service.digital.com and use the
search feature.  For instance, I just searched on 'LSM' and found
   ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/info/SPD/51-24-02.txt
which is a very readable SPD (software product description) of LSM.

The DEC OSF/1� Logical Storage Manager provides the following features:

 o  Data spanning across physical disks (concatenation):

    -  create file systems that span multiple disks to improve I/O per-
       formance and/or create larger file systems

 o  Data mirroring or shadowing (RAID1):

    -  mirror critical data to protect against loss of data and/or to
       improve I/O performance

    -  includes support for root filesystem and swap space mirroring

 o  Data striping (RAID0):

    -  improve I/O performance by interleaving data across multiple disks

 o  Combined mirroring of a striped set (RAID0 and RAID1)

    -  provide data availability and improve system performance

 o  Comprehensive online data storage management functions:

    -  add/remove disks

    -  create/change concatenated, mirrored, and/or striped configu-
       rations

    -  migrate data for disk repair or load balancing

    -  detach a mirror for online back-up with minimal interruption

    -  access mirrored data after a single disk failure and during mir-
       ror merge

    -  monitor performance


As for the layered product stuff, there are some functions that come as
a part of the base, and others as a part of a layered product.  It's a
'teaser' approach - they hope to get you interested enough with the
stuff in the base so that you buy the stuff in the layered product.
The same thing is done for AdvFS.

>3 AdvFS ?
>I think this is a journaling file system (like Veritas?). Is this
>true? How might I use this to good effect with the stuff above?. 

AdvFS is a journaling file system.  It is best used to for quicker
reboots (in the rare :-) occurance that the system needs a reboot)
and for increasing the size of a file system without having to
back it up.  AdvFS has some features similar to LSM, such as
mirroring and concatination, but I have been told that the LSM
implementations of these functions are better.

