In article <katcherD6sIA9.9qt@netcom.com>,
Jeff Katcher <katcher@netcom.com> wrote:
>The Linux release is not yet self-hosting on old hardware and NetBSD only
>supports the old stuff as well.  If this has changed, I'd be happy to be
>corrected. 

I'll speak only for linux here, and specifically "my" port (as opposed
to the 32-bit port done by some DEC people: but we'll be merging soon). 

I personally run linux on the 2000/300 (EISA bus), and it's
"selfhosting" in that I'm able to compile linux under itself and run all
the normal utilities (GNU fileutils, shell-utils, make, uemacs etc).  It
also runs (with additional patches that I haven't yet integrated) on the
noname PCI boards (the same person also reports that GNU emacs runs fine
on a 8MB machine ;-).

It does _not_ run on the older DEC bus platforms (TurboChannel?
Whatever), and probably never will unless some drug-crazed hacker starts
writing drivers.. 

It is by no means complete yet: I haven't enabled any networking stuff
(and it wouldn't work out-of-the-box anyway).  I also haven't done any
user-level development apart from fixing gcc bugs, and I'm using
exclusively OSF/1 binaries for the initial testing (yes, it's OSF/1
binary compatible, although I won't add support for some of the
OSF/1-specific stuff that I'm not interested in).  So it's not
standalone, and won't be for a while. 

I suspect it will be "real" (ie start running XFree86/axp and be
standalone) in a few more months (say, half a year).  It works as-is,
but frankly, you don't want to even try to install it unless you already
have OSF/1 and you want to do kernel and/or other hacking on it. 

		Linus



