>> I wrote a commentary a month or so ago in my newsletter.  The basic 
>> premise is that DEC ought to promote the sale of el cheapo AXP 
>> motherboards together with Linux or NetBSD.  Result: an AXP econobox that 
>> won't compete directly with existing AXP products.  This concept is being 
>> championed by some people in the Digital Unix group.  Accordingly, if 
>> such a package seems interesting to you, indicate so in a followup message!

Greetings,

You may be interested to know that Digital has manufactured a baby AT form 
factor PC mother board using the Alpha 21066 (166Mhz) CPU for the embedded 
and real time market.  The board sports a PCI local bus, IDE, SCSI-2, floppy,
parallel and 2 serial interfaces.  It has four 16 bit ISA slots, two PCI slots
and one additional slot that can be used for either.  Versions are available
with the larger AT style keyboard connector or the smaller PS2 keyboard and
mouse connectors.  

The AXPpci33 is sold with the 166Mhz processor and NO memory.  It uses garden 
variety SRAM cache chips (15 or 20ns) for a total of 0, 256K or 1Meg.  There
are four PS2 type (72 pin, 70ns, 36Bit) SIMM memory slots arranged in two
logical banks.  (This means you have to use two SIMMs of the same size in each
bank)  The board can support up to 256M of main memory using four 64M SIMMs.

Digital packages the AXPpci33 with three different software configurations. 


	EBP20-BA	Packaged with WNT FW, PS2 KBD, Chip	$ 1,170
	EBP20-BX	Packaged with OSF1 FW, PS2 KBD, Chip	$ 2,165
				- 4 OSF/1 licenses
		QL-MT4AE-6Q		- 2-user
		QL-0NWAE-AA		- Developers kit
		QL-MT6AE-AA		- Server Extensions
		QL-MT5AE-AA		- C Kit
	EBP20-BZ	Packaged with OSF1 FW, PS2 KBD, Chip	$ 1,865
		QL-MT4AE-6Q	- 2-user OSF/1 license only

Please note that the console firmware installed at the factory differs based
on your purchase choice of WindowsNT (WNT) or OSF.  Also note that you only
get the software license when you purchase one of the OSF configurations.  The
OSF media kit must be purchased separately.  The WNT license and media are 
available at any PC software shop.  Just ask for the standard WNT kit and you 
will find a top level directory named Alpha...

Device support is as always a function of the operating system.  WindowsNT
support for the Alpha is extensive and you should look at the support list 
included with your WNT kit for the details.  OSF supported devices are listed
in the OSF software product description (SPD).  A SCSI based cdrom drive must
be used to install either operating system on the AXPpci33 board.  The Digital
RRD43 drive (~$270) is highly recommended for both.

Digital sells the AXPpci33 card in small volumes (less than 100 per year) 
both through DEC Direct and authorized distributors.  The list includes 
Pioneer, Wyle and others.  You might try calling DEC-Direct at 1-800-DIGITAL.


-- 
Bob Segrest




