X-Deja-AN: 100181192
organization: Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

My 3000/300LX (64 MB) was just upgraded from OSF/1 V2.0 to V3.2. After the
upgrade I thought someone had swapped a 386 CPU in while they were at it.
Performance was terrible and the system seemed to hang when I attempted to
build the gcc stage 2 compiler. The disk was getting pounded and monitor showed
a lot of paging even though over 10 Meg of memory was free.

Our Digital consultant found the solution, but did not have any explanation for
it. Just a note in an internal conference. I added the lines:
VM:
ubc-borrowpercent=20

to "/etc/sysconfigtab".

After a reboot I was back to normal and gcc built just fine.

Others here have seen the same effect and this change has made them MUCH
happier. But I still have 2 questions:

1. What does this change do? I assume it allows or limits the use of the ubc,
but I don't know which and I don't know if 20 is increasing or reducing the
default. Any idea what is going on here?

2. Is this specific to my configuration (the other systems were identical
configurations to mine). I'd think I would have heard complaints about this
since it was VERY noticeable!

