In Article <3mc0mg$j57@amhux3.amherst.edu>
pdchapin@unix.amherst.edu (PAUL D. CHAPIN) writes:
>After some of you have had time to play with Satan, the rest of use
>probably would be interested in what you find.  Frankly, I'm not wild
>about running a program whose stated purpose is to find holes in
>system security until it has some kind of track record.

An excellent suggestion. CIAC and CERT have already posted that running SATAN
will CREATE a security problem on the system running it. This is supposed to be
fixed by the yet-to-be-released V1.1 code. The problem is only present while
SATAN is running, but anyone running courtney or any other intrusion detector
could well exploit the hole while SATAN is still running.

In general, SATAN is a nice tool, but it does take a little common sense.

By default it only tests the system on which it is run via the loopback
interface, but can be easily set up to test any remote system or entire nets.
The results are stored in a database and my be easily reviewed. There are
excellent tutorials on the various security problems SATAN looks for and how to
fix them.

I am also happy to say that SATAN spotted no problems my system, even when in
heavy attack mode. But if I run it from any host from which there is a
host-based trust relationship (R-suite, Xhost, NFS, etc.), it will find and
report. The bottom line is that simple host based security is probably always a
bad idea.

