Thomas Leitner (tom@finwds01.tu-graz.ac.at) wrote:
: Thomas Leitner (tom@finwds01.tu-graz.ac.at) wrote:

: : I found that neither in the FAQ nor in the recent postings here so:
: : I need to get the hardware serial number under OSF/1. Apparently the
: : hostid command can not be used for that. The sysinfo system call
: : won't help either.

: : Question: Can someone please post me a short code fragment how to
: : get the hardware serial number under OSF/1?

: Aehmm, well. Am I touching some secret here or is there really no way
: to get the CPU (or system) serial number somehow? I, as well could
: use the ethernet address for my purposes. How can I get out the
: ethernet address of the machine from a C program (if possible
: a non-priviledged)?

Thanks to John Kohl <jtk@atria.com>. He mailed me the following 
routine for getting the ethernet addr. (slightly modified by me). It 
even works under a non-priviledged account:

#include <stdio.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <sys/ioctl.h>
#include <net/if.h>

main()
{
        int s;
        struct ifreq *ifrq, ifreqs[32];
        struct ifdevea eaddr;
        struct ifconf ifc;
        long l1, l2;

        memset((char *)ifreqs, 0, sizeof(ifreqs));

        s = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
        ifc.ifc_req = ifreqs;
        ifc.ifc_len = sizeof(ifreqs);
        ioctl(s, SIOCGIFCONF, &ifc);
        for (ifrq = ifreqs;
             ifc.ifc_len > 0;
             ifrq++, ifc.ifc_len -= sizeof(*ifrq)) {
            /* for each known interface, try to get a physaddr. */
            strncpy(eaddr.ifr_name, ifrq->ifr_name, IFNAMSIZ);
            if (ioctl(s, SIOCRPHYSADDR, &eaddr) == 0) {
                /* eaddr now has address. */
                break;                  /* from for loop */
            }
        }
        close(s);
        printf("name='%s'\n", eaddr.ifr_name);
        printf("def_pa='%02x:%02x:%02x:%02x:%02x:%02x'\n",
             eaddr.default_pa[0],
             eaddr.default_pa[1],
             eaddr.default_pa[2],
             eaddr.default_pa[3],
             eaddr.default_pa[4],
             eaddr.default_pa[5]);
        printf("cur_pa='%02x:%02x:%02x:%02x:%02x:%02x'\n",
             eaddr.current_pa[0],
             eaddr.current_pa[1],
             eaddr.current_pa[2],
             eaddr.current_pa[3],
             eaddr.current_pa[4],
             eaddr.current_pa[5]);
}

Thanks again -- Tom
