In article <3leb5g$72a@taurus.fccc.edu>, Michel van der List <M_vanderList@fccc.edu> writes:
|>Hi there.
|>
|>I was looking through the man pages to try to figure out how
|>sigaction works. Most things are pretty straight-forward, but
|>one thing is eluding me. I'm reading about the sa_flags and the
|>SA_SIGINFO bit:
|>
|>	If this bit is cleared and the signal is caught, the
|>	argument specified by the signal parameter is passed as
|>	the only argument to the signal catching function. Two
|>	additional arguments are also available. The second
|>	argument provides an exception-specific code defined in
|>	<sys/signal.h>; this value is always zero (0) for
|>	nonexception signals. The third argument is a pointer
|>	to a sigcontext structure.
|>
|>So how many arguments do I get passed to my handler? One or
|>three? What does it mean when 'the signal parameter is passed
|>as the only argument', but 'Two additional arguments are also
|>available'? Can somebody enlighted me? Does somebody have some
|>example code to get to the sigcontext structure through signal
|>handling?

I believe that language has been cleaned up a bit recently.

Basically, the handler is always passed 3 arguments. You can access
as many as you declare for your signal handler, e.g.:

	void
	handler(int sig)

	void
	handler(int sig, int code, struct sigcontext *scp)

	Etc.

Two events under user control determine the nature of the 2nd and 3rd
arguments:

Argument 2:

   If SA_SIGINFO is set for the signal via sigaction(), the second
   argument is a siginfo_t *:

   void
   handler(int sig, siginfo_t *sip, struct sigcontext *scp)

   If SA_SIGINFO is not set, the second argument is an int code:

   void
   handler(int sig, int code, struct sigcontext *scp)


Argument 3:

   If the application was built with our System V environment (linked
   against libsys5), the third argument becomes a pointer to a ucontext_t:

   void
   handler(int sig, int OR siginfo_t *, ucontext_t *ucp)

   This holds true for our Spec 1170 (XPG4.2) implementation, which
   chose to follow the SVID3 specification.

So, what's in the 2nd and 3rd args is independent, and the user has
control over the nature of each one.

Hope that helps.




