In article <3lumrd$obu@canopus.cc.umanitoba.ca>,
    fee@access.mbnet.mb.ca (Everett Fee) writes:
|>1. Connecting to the Internet. How do I register my machine with a domain
|>name server? I can ping my DNS successfully but can't get outside it.

In general this sounds like a local matter, which may take some digging
around on your part.  If you're pinging a BIND server then I guess you've
configured your IP stack properly for your local subnet, so start by
asking whoever gave you the IP address if they know who does the DNS
administration for your organization, as you'll want to be registered
in your local domain.  Then keep asking questions...eventually you'll
find yourself off-LAN!

|>2. Access to files and printers on the LAN, a VAX-based server running
|>DECnet.

If you've got the LP CDROM (and associated licenses) you may want to
consider running DECnet/OSI on your OSF/1 system.  This will let you
get at the DECnet-VAX system...I believe the docs are on the CDROM in
Bookreader format.  Alternatively, you could ask the local network admin
folks if there is a DECnet/Internet Gateway configured, which would let
you get at DECnet-only systems from an IP-only system (and vice versa).
Or you could ask the VMS system manager why they aren't running TCP/IP...

In any event, good luck!

Dan Harrington
DECnet/OSI Engineering



