Just about any workstation comes with two RS232 ports, but I need two
more.  Not for printers, modems, or terminals, but for some
specialized equipment that can only be controlled over RS232 lines.
I have to control up to four such devices from a single workstation.

What's the easiest way to add another couple of serial ports?  The Alpha
would have some spare ISA slots, and there are lots of EISA boards
with two, four or more RS232 ports --- for Intel PC's.  Is it an easy
thing to just plug one of these into the Alpha ISA bus and use it?  
Can OSF/1 just recognize and use such a board as a regular terminal
port, or is it necessary to obtain a special device driver?

It seems DEC itself does not provide an ISA or PCI board for the Alpha
with just a few extra serial ports.  In the online DECdirect catalog I
find something called a CX101 Asynchronous Multiplexer for EISA/ISA
systems, but that has 16 ports and a special cable and an external box
with a big row of sockets on it --- it might work, but it's really a
lot more than I need, and I'd like to avoid adding cables and boxes.
Also the printed DEC PC catalogs mention (but do not show or describe)
something called a multiport ISA host adapter for Intel PC's, which
sounds like it may be the CX101 again.

I would also like to avoid terminal servers --- again, it's more
cables and boxes, and we've found that terminal servers don't work so
well with devices that are not really terminals.

