In article <3m2b4i$3df@jac.zko.dec.com> Peter.Mayne@cao.mts.dec.com (Peter Mayne) writes:

pjdm> In article <5jKm9tT_WkB@marzen.saar.de>,
pjdm> hm@marzen.saar.de (Holger Marzen) wrote:

>> you told me to insert a 'ptys'-line to /etc/securesys (thank you).
>> Now I can remote login as root, but I can't remote login as root if I run  
>> an PC-X-Server and get the osf-welcome screen via XDM.

pjdm> I don't know, but given that securettys has the line

pjdm> :0

pjdm> in it (from memory), I'd *guess* that you'd put in the line

pjdm> :pcname

*NO*.  X display names for remote hosts are of the following form:

	hostname:0

IF the host is in the same sub-domain, and

	f.q.d.n:0

if not.

For example, assume a server of Orb.Nashua.NH.US, and X-terminals
at onexterm.Nashua.NH.US and anotherxterm.Hollis.NH.US, and that
for some silly reason I really want to allow those two remote
sites to log in directly as root on Orb.  My /etc/securettys
would then contain:

	onexterm:0
	anotherxterm.Hollis.NH.US:0

I hope that's clear enough.

--
