>Does anyone know what/how one gets this bloody SATAN interface?  I thought it
>was a URL you went in through to test a network, but someone says there a
>local client you can run too - anybody know where to get it?  Thanks.

Satan is a utility for looking at network security. It has pointed out several
potential problems on my home system. It uses HTML for its user interface and I
suppose that's where the confusion lies. But you don't execute satan from a
browser. You run satan as you would any executable and IT forks off the
browser.

SATAN requires C, Perl 5, and a web browser, Mosaic or netscape preferred, but
Lynx is supposed to work.

The Alpha is not explicitly supported, but I built it under OSF/1 V3.2 using
'make freebsd' and have had no problems. You need to run SATAN as root, so if
you don't have root access on your system, don't bother building it.

Here is the list of sites posted by the authors as of Monday:

ftp://ftp.mcs.anl.gov/pub/security
ftp://vixen.cso.uiuc.edu/security/satan-1.0.tar.Z
ftp://ftp.denet.dk/pub/security/tools/satan/satan-1.0.tar.Z
