In article <D6Lw6v.HFq@ncgrafix.demon.co.uk>, steve@ncgrafix.demon.co.uk
(steve) wrote:

> How do I tell the X server how big my screen is such that these 
> functions return correct values?

The XDec server supports a command line option that specifies the
resolution of the display, in pixels/inch.  So if your display is 1024x768
pixels, and the physical size of the image area of your monitor is
300mmx225mm, then your horizontal and vertical resolution is
1024/(300/25.4) = 86.7dpi.

If you start your server with the option

    X -dpi 87

(either by hand or from Xdm in /usr/lib/X11/xdm/Xservers), it should then
report the physical attributes correctly.

If you have different horizontal and vertical resolutions, you can specify
these as

   X -dpix 87 -dpiy 89

but I'd probably try to tweak the display controls a bit to get dpix ==
dpiy, as there are probably lots of apps out there that think that pixels
are square :-)

pch



