johnston@nrc.ca (Daniel F. Johnston (Dan)) wrote:
>
> I have a user with an Alpha 3000/900, 256 meg. of memory,
> 200 meg swap on system disk, 300 meg swap on user disk, and
> a very LARGE array to model (FEA analysis).  We have installed
> the latest OSF/1 (3.2).
> 
> The problem is that the OS seems to have been configured (by
> default) for 128 meg per user.  I need to allow him to use as
> much of the above virtual space as possible, and we can add more
> memory and an another swap disk if necessary.  We need at least
> 500 meg of virtual space for that user (to start).
> 
> OK ... so how do I change that limit on OSF/1.  I found 'ulimit'
> but I cannot find any information on it except for the man page
> on th eprogram call.  I have all the documentation on CD, tell
> me were to look.  How do I balance the max physical memory and
> max virtual memory parameters to give the user his 0.5-1 gig
> virtual space?
> 
> Thanks for any help.
> Dan.Johnston@nrc.ca
> 
> 

The right command to change this depends on what shell you are using.
The default user limits look like this (displayed by the C-shell
"limit" command.

cputime         unlimited
filesize        unlimited
datasize        131072 kbytes
stacksize       2048 kbytes
coredumpsize    unlimited
memoryuse       507904 kbytes
descriptors     4096 files
addressspace    1048576 kbytes

In C-shell, the "unlimit" command will set all these values to
the maximum allowed by the system:

cputime         unlimited
filesize        unlimited
datasize        1048576 kbytes
stacksize       32768 kbytes
coredumpsize    unlimited
memoryuse       507904 kbytes
descriptors     4096 files
addressspace    1048576 kbytes

In the Bourne and Korn shells, use the "ulimit" command, with a specific
number.  To set the datasize to 1Gb, for example, do this:

ulimit -d 1048576

It is possible to change both the initial default values and
the maximum allowable values, but not on a per-user basis.
The default and maximum values may be set by putting entries
in /etc/sysconfigtab -- this is described in appendix A
of the "System Tuning and Performance Management" manual, which
is on your Operating System CD.   Note that if you increase
datasize above 1GB, you must also increase addressspace.


		George Chaltas



