In article <3lr6lj$2cv@alpha.cisi.unige.it>, debe@PROBLEM_WITH_INEWS_DOMAIN_FILE (Marco De Benedetto) writes:
|>Hi,
|>I'm trying to use a *very* litte HD to boot an Alpha with OSF1 3.1,
|>the HD is an RZ23L (128MB), so I can't put /usr on it.
|>
|>I'd like to know if it is possible to mount /usr via nfs from another
|>machine, and if the answer is positive how can I do it ( Can I put it in
|>fstab? ).
|>

Sure, you can do it.  Just add the command to mount /usr via NFS in the
/sbin/bcheckrc file.  You'll add it right after the line that causes root to be
mounted read-write.

You'll notice that a mount command for /usr is already in place.  However, it
is used for dataless clients and wouldn't be invoked in your case.  You could
simply remove the test which decides whether to execute this command so that it
does execute every time.  To use the command that's already there, you'll have
to add an entry to /etc/fstab.

Good luck!

Dave Parker
Digital Equipment Corp.



