In article <3ls57d$rdu@ndlc.occ.uky.edu>, ross@ndlc.occ.uky.edu (Ross Hayden) writes:
|>
|>I can't seem to get the system to automatically come up to anything but
|>single user mode, despite my changes to /etc/inittab.  I'm now almost
|>certain there is a parameter I must change at the >>> prompt, but having
|>no documentation yet makes it difficult to know what to change.
|>
|>If anyone could help me change this so that the system boots to run level
|>three by default I would be very appreciative!
|>

It's possible that you have the console variable "boot_osflags" set incorrectly.
If it is set to "s", that would cause the boot to stop in single-user mode.

Setting it to "A" might be the answer to your problem.

The only other thing I can think of is in the /etc/inittab file.  The first
uncommented line should look like:

is:3:initdefault:

If the "3" was replaced with an "s", that would also explain why you come up to
single-user mode.

Dave Parker
Digital Equipment Corp.



