In article <3lsfrs$109i@unix1.sncc.lsu.edu>, pradeep@phzeus.phys.lsu.edu (Pradeep Balachandran) writes:
|>
|>Hi:
|>
|>I have been having this problem recently on an Alpha running osf/1 2.0.
|>When I log into the machine from a different node, it comes up with this
|>message "Too many users logged in, try again later" and prevents a login.
|>Has this got to do with the max. number of users in the configuration
|>file in /sys/conf directory.  This message occurs at times when there are
|>no users logged on the system.  Any suggestions are welcome.
|>
|>Thanx.
|>

Odds are that somebody removed the /upgrade file.  Have a look on your system
to check this.  If this is the case, you should have a /upgrade0 file.  Simply
copy that to /upgrade.  That should resolve the problem.

If /upgrade0 is gone as well, try to find an /upgrade file from another system
(running something earlier than Version 3.0) and copy it over.  If that isn't an
option, you'll have to extract the /upgrade file from a backup or from the 
Version 2.0 distribution media.

Assuming you have to do the latter, and you have the CDROM mounted at /mnt, do
the following:

zcat /mnt/ALPHA/BASE/OSFBASE200 | tar xvf - ./upgrade

I'm assuming that /upgrade is in OSFBASE200.  I'm not positive that it is.

Dave Parker
Digital Equipment Corp.



