   would copy the partition byte-by-byte, so I found this a little
   puzzling.


   If you could shed some light on the above problems, or suggest a
   better method I'd appreciate it.

Hello George,

we don't have as much Alphas than you do, but what we did is
establishing one spare root partitions on a seperate disk for each
server. This root partition can be booted in case of hardware problems
on the "primary boot disk".

All we did is 

-newfs on the spare partition (which must be the "a" partition of a
disk)
-mount this partition, e.g. on /mnt
-making a mirror copy of / on /mnt with *dump*:
	dump -0f - / | (cd /mnt; restore -xf -)
-adapting /mnt/etc/fstab

The partition mounted on /mnt is now ready to be booted.

This works well for our purposes. Perhaps this information may be
useful to you.

