atlas@parcom.ernet.in (Atul Deuskar) writes:

>hi all,	
>	We have 2 DEC 2100 servers running DEC OSF/1 3.0.
>Both have 64MB RAM and 128MB swap allocated. However even 
>with a low user load, after sometime the machines run
>out of swap and then totally hang. A reboot seems to be the
>only escape out. This is even if the user application is
> not memory intensive.
>Any idea on how to avoid this will be useful.

>Thanks a lot.

I had a similar problem here. One of our alphas (128MB memory, 2x128MB
swap) was constantly short of swap space, while all the other machines
were just fine.

After checking the man-page of swapon I discovered that on this one
machine a link /sbin/swapdefault -> /dev/rz3b (=swap1) existed,
forcing the system into the default immediate swap mode. Removing this
link will force the system to over-commitment mode, which allocates
swap space only when the system needs to write a modified virtual page
to the swap space.

The other machines did not have this link. Unfortunately I cannot
tell you, if removing this link helped since I could not reboot the
machine so far. But I am pretty sure it will help.

Wolfram

