In article <3lpjq8$r07@bcarh8ab.bnr.ca> danr@bnr.ca (Dan Reynolds) writes:

Dan> To obtain thread safe code one must remove or protect references to the 
Dan> following dependencies:

Dan> 	o REFERENCES TO GLOBAL ERRNO

Dan> The problem is that the GLOBAL ERRNO is used by many UNIX
Dan> functions, such as file I/O, sockets, semaphores, etc...,
Dan> and even the PTHREAD library.

Umm, look again.  If you read through errno.h, you'll some #if's
for threaded code.  It turns out that, for threaded code, errno
is a macro, and the real error number value is in a per-thread
data area.  Just be sure you built your threaded code correctly.
Use something like this:

	cc ${CFLAGS} -c ${SRCS} -threads

The main thing this does for .o files is add -D_REENTRANT to the
flags passed to cfe.  For final linking, though:

	cc ${LDFLAGS} -o $@ ${OBJS} ${LIBS} -threads

That's where the rest of the magic (to use the right libraries)
happens.  It can (and will) change from release to release, so
don't fake it out.
