newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

In <3lgv2s$s2f@nrcnet0.nrc.ca> johnston@nrc.ca (Daniel F. Johnston (Dan)) writes:

>The problem is that the OS seems to have been configured (by
>default) for 128 meg per user.  I need to allow him to use as
>much of the above virtual space as possible, and we can add more
>memory and an another swap disk if necessary.  We need at least
>500 meg of virtual space for that user (to start).
>
>OK ... so how do I change that limit on OSF/1.  I found 'ulimit'
>but I cannot find any information on it except for the man page
>on th eprogram call.  I have all the documentation on CD, tell
>me were to look.  How do I balance the max physical memory and
>max virtual memory parameters to give the user his 0.5-1 gig
>virtual space?

In a csh compatible shell, you can fix the problem with a single
command:

    ues5:~ 165> limit
    cputime         unlimited
    filesize        unlimited
    datasize        131072 kbytes
                    ^^^^^^
    stacksize       2048 kbytes
    coredumpsize    unlimited
    memoryuse       58776 kbytes
    descriptors     4096 
    ues5:~ 166> limit datasize unlimited
    ues5:~ 167> limit
    cputime         unlimited
    filesize        unlimited
    datasize        1048576 kbytes
                    ^^^^^^^
    stacksize       2048 kbytes
    coredumpsize    unlimited
    memoryuse       58776 kbytes
    descriptors     4096 

If you want more than 1GB of virtual memory for a single process, you'll
have to reconfigure your kernel.  

Other shells might have similar commands for dealing with the limits.
Check the man page of your shell.

Dan
