Hi Thomas,

Thomas.Meyer (Thomas.Meyer@dla-marbach.de) wrote:
: In article <3kjbpe$ah3@fstgds15.tu-graz.ac.at> tom@finwds01.tu-graz.ac.at (Tom Leitner) writes:

:    I just changed my workstation from a DecStation 5000/33 running Ultrix 4.4:    I just changed my workstation from a DecStation 5000/33 running Ultrix 4.4
:    to a DEC 3000/600 running OSF/1. Everything runs great except for one thing:
:    I just can't find an InfoServer access software for OSF/1. For Ultrix
:    I found it in our DEC campus software distribution and I can happily mount
:    InfoServer CDs with it.

:    Question: Is something like that available for OSF/1 and if yes where?

: Dear Tom,

: we have a similar problem: for our PC-LAN with OSF/1 servers we bought an InfoServer with 7 CDROM drives, not knowing about the fact that the InfoServer offers only its proprietary LAD/LAST protocols.

: Although we are able to access the InfoServer from PC-clients, in some cases we would prefer to mount the CDROM drives on our OSF/1 servers and to export them as LAN-Manager shares, because this would prevent MSCDEX.EXE from eating away nearly 50KB of memory (lower memory is a crucial thing on some PCs).

: Because of this we made a lot of investigations, but finally had to find out that the InfoServer is currently *not* able to cooperate with OSF/1. Unfortunately, according to DEC's people, this will not change in the near future.

: The InfoServer obviously still is one of the remnants of DECs past age...

Well, I finally solved it: I got another DecStation running Ultrix 4.4 in 
the network. This DecStation mounts the InfoServer CDs (there is an
InfoServer access software for Ultrix) and just exports them via NFS.
My OSF/1 box, then, NFS mounts the appropriate directories on the
DecStation. 

Works like a charm. So if you got an old Ultrix box (may even be a
VAX running Ultrix/VAX) in your network, you can do it like that.

Tom

