small files, AdvFS can run out of metedata space (inode tables), causing
erroneous 'out of disk space' errors.  Examples of such systems are
Internet News servers and mail servers.

To avoid this problem, preallocate the metedata immediately after the
fileset is created.  This can be done by writing a script that creates
and then deletes the estimated number of files that are expected to
exist in the AdvFS domain (the files created by the script should be
empty).  For example, the following ksh script preallocates the metedata
for 1000 files:

integer f=1000

   while (( f > 0 ))
   do
       touch prealloc_$f
       f=f-1
       done

rm prealloc_*

This ends what is there.

In the OSF/1 V3.2 release notes, on pages 4-16 and 4-17, in
section 4.4.2.10 Increase metadata extent size for large number of
files, we can find (more than what I have included here):

"For this release, a new flag (-x), has been added to the mkfdmn command
that allows you to increase the size of the metadata table extents.  The
syntax for this flag is as follows:

mkfdmn -x number_pages device domain_name

You can increase the number of pages to any value.  The following are
guidelines for the number of pages to allocate:

  o For 50,000 files (of fewer) use the default, 128.
  o For 50,000 to 100,000 files use -x 256.
  o For 100,000 to 200,000 files use -x 512.
  o For 200,000 files (or more) use -x 1024.

Table 4-1 provides the approximate number of pages that your metadata
will occupy given the number of files.  The progression is linear.

Table 4-1

   Number of Files                    Metadata size (8192 byte pages)

       50,000                                 3600
      100,000                                 7200
      200,000                                14400
      400,000                                28800

This ends what is in the 3.2 release notes that I am putting here.

I have kept my server as a test since I had heard/read about this
problem.  I had also heard/read about the "fix" in 3.2.  I am going to
update to V3.2 before I make my server live and move to the 2 GB disk
instead of a 300 MB or so partition.  Hope this helps someone else out
there.

   Mark

