>sleep(1)

>at the end of the CGI script. This works for me.

My solution was a bit more drastic -- patch the HTScript.c source code to
have it ignore the error.  The function is HTLoadScriptResult.  Line 208 is
the one I commented out (the 'break' right after CTRACE(stderr,"HTLoad....")).

I tried telling the software to ignore SIGPIPE but that did not seem to do
anything.  This solution is a tad bit dangerous because it now ignores _all_
errors, but at least the debugging output is still there so you can see
what's going on if you look.

